  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY NIGHT THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 1225 AM MST Mon Nov 16 2026\n\nBreezy and warmer weather expected on Tuesday, as next western\nU.S. upper trough moves into the Great Basin by afternoon. \nPlains will see readings deep into the 60s Tue, with a couple\nnear 70 degf readings in the lower Arkansas Valley. A few \nshowers will reach the San Juans toward sunset, though daytime \nprecip amounts will be light. Trough moves quickly through \nColorado Tuesday night, then lifts rapidly into the nrn plain \nby Wed afternoon. Pattern produces only a brief window of upward\nmotion Tue night, with the best moisture/lift favoring the \neastern San Juans. Could see around 6 inches of snow at Wolf \nCreek Pass before snow tapers off quickly Wed morning, with only\na couple inches over the central mountains and higher peaks of \nthe Sangres. Remaining high terrain and valleys will see a few \nquick moving showers Tue night, with more wind (gusts 45-55 mph)\nthan rain/snow at most locations. I-25/plains will miss precip \nentirely, with just a period of strong winds (gusts 35-45 mph) \novernight into Wed morning. Winds then diminish Wed afternoon as\nupper trough quickly departs and surface gradient weakens. Mid \nlevel cold advection will drop Wed high temps 5-10 degf from \nTuesday's numbers over the mountains, while deep mixing on the \nplains limits the amount of cooling, with readings east of I-25 \nstill into the 60s. \n\nDry and mild on Thu, then the next windy upper trough moves \nthrough the area Thu night into Fri night. Models continue with \na fast/northerly trend with this system, which will again keep \nmost of the accumulating snow limited to areas along the \nContinental Divide (ern San Juans favored). Remaining high \nterrain will see a round of windy showers on Friday, but precip\nlooks light at this point. Trough will bring strong winds to \nall of the area Fri, with gusts over 60 mph possible across the\nmountains, and in the 40-60 mph range over most valleys and \nacross the plains. Dry and mild weather then returns for the \nweekend ahead of still another upper trough moving toward the \nRockies early next week.",
